const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is UAE customs clearance?',
    a: 'UAE clearance converts the CIF ×1.1025 duty+VAT assessment into a release — for Dubai that is Mirsal 2 — plus inspection and any HS-code permit. For regulated goods it also depends on the right HS code and any required import permit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are required for UAE customs clearance?',
    a: 'Four documents form the standard set — commercial invoice, packing list, transport document and certificate of origin — and the HS code sits upstream of all of them. Classify the goods with the correct HS code first, because duty, VAT and any permit requirements all flow from it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is Mirsal 2?',
    a: 'Mirsal 2 is Dubai Customs’ single-window system for filing declarations and paying the 5% duty + 5% VAT assessment. The research snapshot names the system but does not document the full step-by-step workflow, so process details are marked LOW confidence — confirm current steps with Dubai Customs or your broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a customs broker in the UAE?',
    a: 'A broker is not a legal requirement — 0 of the 4 standard documents requires one — but most first-time importers use one. A broker can file Mirsal 2, coordinate HS classification, manage permit timing and handle inspection exceptions. Experienced importers with a UAE trade licence, staff and systems can self-clear; otherwise the brokerage fee usually buys meaningful risk reduction.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between free zone and mainland clearance?',
    a: 'A mainland consignment pays the ×1.1025 stack (5% duty + 5% VAT) at import, while a designated free-zone entry can hold that at 0 until mainland exit. Goods held inside a designated free zone can remain under duty/VAT suspension, but duty and VAT generally apply when they later exit the zone to the mainland market.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a free zone delivery always avoid UAE duty and VAT?',
    a: 'No — suspension holds the ×1.1025 charge at 0 only while goods stay in-zone, and mainland exit generally triggers the 5% duty + 5% VAT. Confirm the zone status and exit treatment with Dubai Customs or your broker before assuming a saving.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does UAE customs clearance cost?',
    a: 'Duty is 5% of CIF and VAT is 5% of CIF+duty, so the tax portion is ×1.1025; brokerage, Mirsal 2 processing, inspection, storage and document fees were not quantified in the verified research snapshot, so request an itemised schedule from your broker. Treat any single “clearance fee” number as indicative, not binding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does UAE customs clearance take?',
    a: 'The verified snapshot publishes 0 reliable hour/day figures for the corridor, so lead time is driven by HS accuracy, document consistency and inspection selection. Complete pre-arrival work first; clearance itself is usually faster than fixing a wrong HS code or missing permit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are the most common UAE clearance hold reasons?',
    a: 'Five failure modes dominate — HS error, invoice/packing mismatch, under-declared value, missing permits and COO inconsistency — and each converts to inspection or hold time. These are operational patterns, not a Dubai Customs policy list — review documents line by line before filing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the UAE require a pre-arrival declaration?',
    a: 'Yes — Mirsal 2 is built for advance filing so the ×1.1025 assessment can clear before discharge and before the 3–7 day demurrage clock starts. File early and resolve exceptions before free storage or demurrage time runs out.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is UAE import duty and VAT calculated?',
    a: 'Duty is assessed on the CIF value (cost, insurance and freight) at a 5% baseline under the GCC Common External Tariff. VAT is 5% on the CIF value plus customs duty. Keep the invoice value, freight and insurance lines consistent across documents to avoid revaluation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a de minimis exemption for UAE imports?',
    a: 'Secondary sources record goods valued at AED 1,000 or less as duty/tax free and gifts up to AED 3,000 as exempt, so crossing AED 1,000 can flip a parcel from 0 to the ×1.1025 stack. Confirm the current threshold with Dubai Customs or the FTA, especially for low-value e-commerce parcels.',
